WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.349 --> 00:00:16.460
Every time you eat a piece of bread or drink
a glass of beer, you are participating in

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:22.810
what might be (depending on how strict you
are about your definitions) one of the longest-running

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.830
microbiology experiments in human history.

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:33.250
While the earliest scientific studies of microbes
usually go back only a few centuries

00:00:33.250 --> 00:00:35.440
when it comes to yeast, the microbe

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.960
at the core of some of our favorite foods
and drinks, well...those past few centuries

00:00:40.960 --> 00:00:43.579
barely scratch the surface.

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:49.629
But before we get to that history, let’s
start with some of the basic biology.

00:00:49.629 --> 00:00:54.559
Yeast are a fungus, though unlike many in
that kingdom, they don’t grow the branching

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:57.960
hyphae that characterize organisms like mushrooms.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:05.339
Instead, yeast grow primarily through a process
of asexual reproduction called “budding.”.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:11.950
While there are many yeast species found among
different fungal phyla, perhaps the most well-known

00:01:11.950 --> 00:01:19.040
is Saccharomyces cerevisiae, which you might
see labelled as brewer’s yeast or baker’s yeast.

00:01:19.040 --> 00:01:24.540
Along with a few other yeasts, these various
strains of S. cerevisiae are what we’ve

00:01:24.549 --> 00:01:31.880
come to rely on for our beer, wine, and bread--and
all through the magic of fermentation.

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:38.719
Yeast, like many organisms, rely on different
metabolic pathways to break down sugar and

00:01:38.719 --> 00:01:40.500
create energy.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.950
These processes usually rely on oxygen.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:49.149
But to deal with situations where oxygen may
be less readily available or some of their

00:01:49.149 --> 00:01:55.680
key metabolic processes are shut down, yeast
turn to alcoholic fermentation.

00:01:55.680 --> 00:02:01.820
Fermentation helps the yeast get the energy
it needs, but the particular chemical path

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:07.210
they follow also produces ethanol and carbon
dioxide.

00:02:07.210 --> 00:02:13.750
The yeast doesn’t need these byproducts—in
fact, too much ethanol could be toxic for them.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:20.760
But what is waste to a yeast is treasure to
us, and that shift in metabolic activity has

00:02:20.760 --> 00:02:26.920
been making our bread rise and our brains
woozy for millennia.

00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:35.300
Molecular studies of pottery jars found in
China found that they may have held some kind

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:41.420
of fermented beverage as far back as 7000
BCE.

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:47.810
And archaeological evidence has turned up
for leavened bread from 2000 BCE in Egypt

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:50.720
and 1000 BCE in North Western China.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:55.400
It’s easy to see why our use of yeast has
persisted for millennia.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:03:00.320
To quote one group of scientists, it helped
us make food and drinks with a “enriched

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:05.030
sensorial palate” and also a “euphoriant
effect.”

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:12.940
But the long-standing ubiquity of yeast also
emphasizes how mysterious their origins are.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:17.670
We don’t actually know how yeast became
involved with our cooking.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:24.040
We just know that it was ancient, likely starting
with wine, and would come to span continents,

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.950
potentially spreading through trade.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:31.840
The results were food and drinks produced
through a series of observations about how

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:37.450
factors like temperature, time, and air might
affect the final product.

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:44.480
To quote Samuel Johnson’s 18th century dictionary,
barm--a type of yeast--is “the ferment put

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:50.030
into drink to make it work, and into bread
to lighten and swell it.”

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:56.370
At the time they didn’t know what was actually
in yeast or how it worked to shape those results.

00:03:56.370 --> 00:04:00.570
We just knew it was a thing that provided…something…somehow….

00:04:00.570 --> 00:04:07.790
Moreover, the earliest glimpses of yeast under
the microscope seemed to support the idea

00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:13.910
that yeast was just some sort of chemical,
a fermenting agent, not a biological entity

00:04:13.910 --> 00:04:15.320
of its own.

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:19.690
When Antoni van Leeuwenhoek observed yeast
in the 17th century, he thought they were

00:04:19.690 --> 00:04:23.120
just bits of globular particles.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.880
Against the new and incredible bits of life
he was seeing for the first time through the

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:33.510
microscope, yeast seemed useful but ultimately
unliving.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:39.120
But over the next few centuries, as more scientists
worked to try and understand how fermentation

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:46.040
actually worked, they identified not just
the chemical reactions that shaped the process,

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:51.540
but that yeast was an actual living organism
essential to fermentation.

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:56.600
Eventually, Louis Pasteur would put all the
pieces together, publishing a paper titled

00:04:56.600 --> 00:05:02.520
“The Memoir of the Fermentation of Alcohol”,
or something like that, but in French.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:10.220
It was published in 1857 and it established
the interplay between chemistry and biology

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:15.930
that allowed yeast to turn to alcoholic fermentation
to survive.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:21.190
He would also later uncover another form of
fermentation carried out by bacteria, which

00:05:21.190 --> 00:05:24.260
is called lactic acid fermentation.

00:05:24.260 --> 00:05:30.220
Pasteur’s work didn’t just change our
understanding of how bread was made, it would

00:05:30.220 --> 00:05:37.440
go on to inform our understanding of germ
theory, and how the invisible world of microbes

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.460
connects to the grim realities of disease.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.920
From this early history, yeast have gone on
to become perhaps one of the most studied

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:51.420
eukaryotic organisms in modern biological
research.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:56.780
Saccharomyces cerevisiae in particular has
had an illustrious research career.

00:05:56.780 --> 00:06:04.100
In 1996, its genome was fully sequenced, making
it the first eukaryotic organism to be genetically

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:05.830
mapped out.

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:11.270
Part of what makes yeast such great research
organisms is that they are easy to grow and

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:17.150
manipulate--both through environmental and
genetic means--which has allowed us to use

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:22.120
them to study everything from DNA replication
to prion diseases.

00:06:22.120 --> 00:06:28.889
In the same way that cultures have refined
the ways we use yeast in our food and drinks,

00:06:28.889 --> 00:06:33.110
scientists have refined the way we use yeast
in our lab experiments.

00:06:33.110 --> 00:06:38.510
They’ve even developed genetic engineering
techniques that repurpose yeast to brew the

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:44.090
ingredients for everything from perfumes to
antimalarial drugs.

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:49.650
When you consider just how far we’ve come
with yeast, you might begin to wonder: did

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:52.620
we domesticate them?

00:06:52.620 --> 00:06:58.919
After all, our early days of yeast use likely
coincided with our early days of plant and

00:06:58.919 --> 00:07:00.990
animal domestication.

00:07:00.990 --> 00:07:06.560
So in the same way that we’ve come to rely
on domesticated animals for food and labor,

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:11.240
maybe yeast are our microbial workhorses.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:16.980
The answer to that question is one scientists
are still studying, in part because the history

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:25.130
of yeast is so large and mysterious, and in
part because domestication has a very specific

00:07:25.130 --> 00:07:27.699
definition in these conversations.

00:07:27.699 --> 00:07:34.750
One paper we found describes it as “human
selection and breeding of wild species to

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:41.900
obtain cultivated variants that thrive in
man-made environments, but behave sub-optimally

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:47.040
in nature,” now that’s quite a number
of nuanced requirements to check off.

00:07:47.040 --> 00:07:52.790
But with this definition in mind, beer yeast
seems to show the most evidence for having

00:07:52.790 --> 00:07:58.639
been domesticated, which is likely connected
to specific aspects of the brewing process

00:07:58.639 --> 00:08:03.770
that have limited the exposure of the domesticated
yeast to wild yeast.

00:08:03.770 --> 00:08:10.840
But all yeasts considered, the genetic analysis
of beer yeast strains show that this domestication

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:16.770
is actually relatively recent—as in, it
only happened a few centuries ago.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:22.900
While it did happen before we even knew that
yeast was a living thing that we were domesticating,

00:08:22.900 --> 00:08:28.590
it still came long after we started using
yeast to make beer, leaving much of the organism’s

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:32.579
history still a mystery.

00:08:32.579 --> 00:08:38.889
It is often tempting to think that we have
a microbial world and a human world that intersect

00:08:38.889 --> 00:08:42.770
only in chemical reactions and biological
connections.

00:08:42.770 --> 00:08:49.879
But yeast reminds us that microbes are more
than just a scientific reality, they are a

00:08:49.879 --> 00:08:54.779
cultural one--even when we didn’t know they
were around.

00:08:54.779 --> 00:09:02.339
Yeast have created our foods, shaped our traditions,
and both bonded and divided us.

00:09:02.339 --> 00:09:08.480
And how we use and change yeast will only
continue to shape our lives.

00:09:08.480 --> 00:09:15.129
Their future, tied so much to our own, is
every bit as grand and mysterious as their

00:09:15.129 --> 00:09:18.389
past has been.

00:09:18.389 --> 00:09:24.649
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

